  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">OUR FOREST, OUR FUTURE: COMMUNITY-LED AFRICAN FOREST ELEPHANT (LOXODONTA CYCLOTIS) CONSERVATION IN THE BAS OGOOU OF GABON</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE PURPOSE OF THIS PROJECT IS TO SUPPORT A COMMUNITY-DRIVEN ELEPHANT CONSERVATION INITIATIVE IN THE BAS OGOOU LAKE REGION OF GABON (PART OF THE BAS OGOOU RAMSAR SITE), BY STRENGTHENING COMMUNITY FORESTRY MANAGEMENT, ELEPHANT MONITORING, AND ECOTOURISM CAPACITY. GABON IS THE PRIMARY REMAINING STRONGHOLD FOR THE SPECIES, WITH AN ESTIMATED 95,110 ELEPHANTS (OVER HALF THE REMAINING FOREST ELEPHANT POPULATION). THIS PROJECT IS INTENDED TO CONSERVE AFRICAN FOREST ELEPHANTS AND THEIR HABITAT BY ADDRESSING THE IMPACTS OF POACHING AND HUMAN-ELEPHANT CONFLICT (HEC) AND BUILDING A CONSTITUENCY FOR ELEPHANT CONSERVATION. SPECIFIC ACTIVITIES INCLUDE: (1) FACILITATING AND ADVANCING THE OGUEMOU COMMUNITY FORESTRY LEGAL APPLICATION, INCLUDING DEVELOPMENT OF A FOREST MANAGEMENT PLAN AND FOREST ELEPHANT STRATEGY (2) DEPLOYING COMMUNITY FOREST MONITORING PATROLS (3) INSTALLING AND TESTING LOWER SPECIFICATION AND COST-EFFECTIVE ELEPHANT-PROOF FENCING (4) CONDUCTING ENVIRONMENTAL OUTREACH AND EDUCATION PROGRAMMING THROUGH PRIMARY AND SECONDARY SCHOOLS, LOCAL NATURE CLUBS, AND COMMUNITY EVENTS AND (5) BUILDING THE CAPACITY OF COMMUNITY COOPERATIVES TO ENGAGE IN ECOTOURISM THAT PROMOTES ECONOMIC INCENTIVES FOR ELEPHANT CONSERVATION.</narrative>
  </description>
